MESSAGE {"run": "run_e41881834d74", "ordinal": 14, "role": "assistant", "content": "I have all source text. Now writing research.json with verified quotes.", "tool_calls": [{"function": {"arguments": "{\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"The fixed-revision DeepSeek 'dsh-superagent' materials describe a positively-scoped intake pipeline: a Trigger is timestamp-normalized, required to belong to at least one Thread, deduplicated by id, then passed through a SourceScope gate. A trigger the scope does not listen to is still persisted and given a 'filtered' disposition with an empty fanout, so rejection is recorded rather than silent. Listened triggers are recorded, their Threads get monotonic lastAt and a derived symmetric correlation set, and only thread.subscriptions are iterated for fan-out, with context subscriptions delivering 'context: true' inputs unless a ticket is also reached through its own thread. Ended tickets resume only on a human message on a non-context thread, input ids are deterministic, a per-trigger disposition of fanout/context is persisted, Eval is queued only when no ticket owns the trigger, then tickets are woken and trigger.received is emitted. The documented philosophy reinforces this with an attention-cost test, direct-address allowlisting and record-only mode for unrequested work. This is a public research prototype, explicitly not an official product and not production-ready, and explicitly not identical to the implementation described in the architecture article, 'Exploring Super-agent Design: Towards a More Autonomous Agent System'; some article designs are stated future directions not implemented in this prototype, and the article text itself is not among the supplied materials, so article-level statements are directions only. Statements here are source-text inspection, not observed execution. FORGE implications are drawn with two concrete design choices (default-deny scope gate with audited rejection dispositions; subscription passthrough over derived thread correlation rather than new settings) and matching risks.\\\",\\n  \\\"citations\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_readme\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"737997cf5b7d4129f12ed5485ebbdd5e9d2ce3859a82ded6ac535d222327b713\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 3,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"> **Research prototype for reference only, not an official DeepSeek product.** This code grew out of our team's day-to-day development work, with much of the engineering done by coding agents. Treat it as research code, not production-ready software. It may still contain bugs, and its coding style and engineering practices may be inconsistent. Review and adapt it before using it in your own environment.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_readme\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"737997cf5b7d4129f12ed5485ebbdd5e9d2ce3859a82ded6ac535d222327b713\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"> We have adapted it for publication, so it is not identical to our internal version or the implementation described in *Exploring Super-agent Design: Towards a More Autonomous Agent System*. Some designs discussed in the article are directions for future development and are not implemented in this prototype.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_readme\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"737997cf5b7d4129f12ed5485ebbdd5e9d2ce3859a82ded6ac535d222327b713\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 7,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 7,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"dsh-superagent is a proactive coding agent for one GitHub repository, packaged as a bundle for [DeepSeek Harness](https://www.deepseek.com/en/harness/). It listens to GitHub and, optionally, Feishu. When someone allowed asks the agent for work, it opens a work item, works on it in its own agent session and directory, and answers in the original conversation, for example with a pull request. A web panel shows what needs a person.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_philosophy\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"0a12126843556fde2bcc2ca52dae2c297b9b5ca9170c3e33d624b2a4d85ca76b\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 11,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 11,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"- **It acts only when asked, or when a written rule allows it.** A request counts only when it addresses the agent directly and comes from a person the team allowed: one on an allowed list, or anyone in a conversation the team opened to requests. Work nobody asked for needs a rule the team wrote, and starts in a mode that only records what the agent would have done.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_philosophy\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"0a12126843556fde2bcc2ca52dae2c297b9b5ca9170c3e33d624b2a4d85ca76b\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 19,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 20,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"- **Few concepts.** The design has only its domain concepts, such as a Source, and their concrete implementations, such as GitHub and Feishu. A business need becomes an attribute of one of them, or a prompt the agent follows. The design does not add a concept for it.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_intake\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"323aadefea69436ba23ed2de3bdbaad673d61915be9e239f3233ecf551a74179\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 22,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 23,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"    if (!trigger.threads.length) throw new Error('A trigger must belong to at least one thread')\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_intake\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"323aadefea69436ba23ed2de3bdbaad673d61915be9e239f3233ecf551a74179\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 25,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 28,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"    const filter = this.options.scope.scope(trigger)\\\\n    if (!filter.listened) {\\\\n      this.repo.triggers.save(trigger); this.repo.triggers.saveDisposition(trigger.id, { filtered: { code: filter.reason }, fanout: [], at })\\\\n      return 'filtered'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_intake\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"323aadefea69436ba23ed2de3bdbaad673d61915be9e239f3233ecf551a74179\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 37,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 38,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"      thread.correlated = [...new Set([...thread.correlated, ...keys.filter(other => other !== thread.key)])]\\\\n      for (const ticketId of thread.subscriptions) {\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_intake\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"323aadefea69436ba23ed2de3bdbaad673d61915be9e239f3233ecf551a74179\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 42,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 45,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"        const followed = !!thread.context?.includes(ticketId)\\\\n        const resumes = trigger.human && ticket?.ended?.by !== 'human' && !followed\\\\n        if (!ticket || (!liveTicket(ticket) && !resumes)) continue\\\\n        reached.set(ticketId, { ticket, context: followed && (reached.get(ticketId)?.context ?? true) })\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_intake\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"323aadefea69436ba23ed2de3bdbaad673d61915be9e239f3233ecf551a74179\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 50,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 50,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"      this.tickets.addInput(ticket, { id: key('input', ticketId, 'trigger', trigger.id), kind: 'trigger', triggerId: trigger.id, at,\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_intake\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"323aadefea69436ba23ed2de3bdbaad673d61915be9e239f3233ecf551a74179\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 53,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 55,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"    const fanout = [...reached.keys()], context = fanout.filter(id => reached.get(id)!.context)\\\\n    const disposition = { fanout, ...context.length ? { context } : {}, at }\\\\n    this.repo.triggers.saveDisposition(trigger.id, disposition)\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"deepseek_intake\\\",\\n      \\\"source_sha256\\\": \\\"323aadefea69436ba23ed2de3bdbaad673d61915be9e239f3233ecf551a74179\\\",\\n      \\\"line_start\\\": 58,\\n      \\\"line_end\\\": 60,\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"    if (!this.owners(trigger).length) this.queueForEval(trigger)\\\\n    for (const ticketId of fanout) this.tickets.wake(ticketId)\\\\n    this.emit('trigger.received', { triggerId: trigger.id, threads: trigger.threads.map(threadKey) })\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"comparison\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"topic\\\": \\\"Ingress gate: default-deny SourceScope with audited rejection\\\",\\n      \\\"prototype\\\": \\\"receive() normalizes timestamps, throws if a trigger has no thread, returns 'duplicate' on id replay, then asks this.options.scope.scope(trigger); a not-listened trigger is still saved together with a disposition {filtered:{code: reason}, fanout: [], at} and returns 'filtered' (intake L21-L29). Filtering is therefore positive-scope, deterministic and replay-safe at entry.\\\",\\n      \\\"forge_implication\\\": \\\"FORGE should adopt the same shape for external triggers: one deterministic scope decision taken before any model or planner work, with the negative decision persisted as a first-class record (reason code, empty fanout, timestamp) so that silently dropped work is auditable. This is a design choice to keep scope evaluation a pure predicate over typed trigger data, not a prompt-level judgement.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"deepseek_intake\\\", \\\"deepseek_philosophy\\\"]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"topic\\\": \\\"Subscription pass-through and context precedence\\\",\\n      \\\"prototype\\\": \\\"Fan-out iterates only thread.subscriptions; a ticket may subscribe to a thread as context (thread.context), producing inputs flagged context: true; any reach through the ticket's own thread sets context false, because reached.set uses 'followed && (previous ?? true)' (intake L32-L33, L42-L45, L50-L51). Resumption of an ended ticket requires a human trigger, not a human-ended ticket, and not a context-only subscription (L40-L44).\\\",\\n      \\\"forge_implication\\\": \\\"FORGE delivery should distinguish 'ambiently informed' from 'directly tasked'. Carrying an explicit context flag on each delivered work item lets downstream policy (budget, autonomy, response obligation) differ without inventing a new concept, consistent with the few-concepts guidance. Risk-adjusted choice: derive delivery from existing subscription relations on the thread, rather than maintaining a separate fan-out list.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"deepseek_intake\\\", \\\"deepseek_philosophy\\\"]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"topic\\\": \\\"Derived correlation instead of configured routing\\\",\\n      \\\"prototype\\\": \\\"Thread correlation is computed during ingestion as a symmetric union of co-occurring thread keys (thread.correlated, intake L31, L37), and thread.lastAt is advanced monotonically (L36). Nothing in the inspected code configures which threads are related.\\\",\\n      \\\"forge_implication\\\": \\\"FORGE can compute correlation/adjacency as a byproduct of observed co-occurrence rather than a new routing setting, keeping operator configuration to zero and preserving the 'few settings' rule; the same record then supports later retrieval and dedupe without extra schemas.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"deepseek_intake\\\", \\\"deepseek_philosophy\\\"]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"topic\\\": \\\"Durability and side-effect ordering\\\",\\n      \\\"prototype\\\": \\\"Order is persist trigger -> correlate/save threads -> addInputs with deterministic ids -> saveDisposition -> conditional queueForEval -> wake tickets -> emit 'trigger.received' (intake L30-L60). Eval is queued only when no ticket owns the trigger (L56-L58), so a claimed human message is not double-processed, while every bot message and unclaimed human message gets a fallback owner.\\\",\\n      \\\"forge_implication\\\": \\\"FORGE should treat wake-up and event emission as effects that follow durable state, and should have a single explicit unclaimed-input fallback path. A missing fallback would strand or duplicate work; the 'owners(trigger).length === 0' test is the concrete design choice to mirror.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"deepseek_intake\\\"]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"topic\\\": \\\"Prototype scope versus architecture article and unimplemented capability\\\",\\n      \\\"prototype\\\": \\\"The prototype is a coding agent for one GitHub repository, optionally Feishu, with work done in its own session and directory and answered in the original conversation (README L7). README L5 states the publication version is not identical to the architecture article's implementation and that some article designs are future directions not implemented in this prototype; the article text itself is not included in the supplied materials.\\\",\\n      \\\"forge_implication\\\": \\\"FORGE comparisons must not attribute article claims to running code. Multi-repository breadth, autonomous initiation beyond written rules, and any article-described topology should be treated as unevidenced directions; FORGE should prototype them behind the same scope gate and record-only mode rather than assuming a proven mechanism.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"deepseek_readme\\\", \\\"deepseek_philosophy\\\"]\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"risks\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"Silent or unaccountable suppression: a default-deny scope gate can drop legitimate work with no trace, and in the inspected code the filtered path returns before any wake or notice, so an operator relying on the system may never learn that work was ignored.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Require a persisted disposition for every non-delivery (reason code, empty fanout, timestamp), exactly as the filtered branch does, an